<Layout
  title="GPT-6.2 Sol - A.I. - Rycode"
  description="GPT-6.2 Sol, OpenAI’s model for everyday work, released 17 November 2026 at GPT-6.1 Sol’s prices of $2 input, $0.10 cached input, and $10 output per million tokens, with a 1.05M context window."
  variant="ai"
>
  <article class="ai-model">
    <p class="ai-model-kicker">OpenAI · GPT-6.2</p>
    <h1 class="ai-model-title">GPT-6.2 Sol</h1>
    <p class="ai-model-dek">GPT-6.2 Sol is OpenAI’s “efficient, versatile model for everyday work,” released 17 November 2026 alongside <a href="/ai/models/openai/gpt-6-2-astra/">GPT-6.2 Astra</a>, per <a href="https://openai.com/index/introducing-gpt-6-2-astra-sol/">Introducing GPT-6.2 Astra and Sol</a> [1]. OpenAI says that compared with <a href="/ai/models/openai/gpt-6.1-sol/">GPT-6.1 Sol</a>, it is “smarter, more efficient, and lets you complete more tasks on the same paid plan” [1]. The API id, the name a program uses to call the model, is <code>gpt-6.2-sol</code> [1][2]. Context is 1,050,000 tokens, with up to 922,000 input tokens and 128,000 output tokens, per the <a href="https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/models/gpt-6.2-sol">GPT-6.2 Sol API model page</a> [2]. It keeps GPT-6.1 Sol’s API prices: $2 per million input tokens, $0.10 per million cached input tokens, and $10 per million output tokens [1][2]. Tokens are the word pieces models are billed by.</p>
    <hr class="ai-model-rule" />
    <ModelFacts />
    <h2 class="ai-model-section">Training</h2>
    <p>Parameter count, training compute, dataset, and training method are not in the sources on this page. The API model page lists a knowledge cutoff of 1 June 2026 [2].</p>
    <h2 class="ai-model-section">Inference</h2>
    <p>The API model page lists text and image input and text output, tool use such as web search and computer use through the Responses API, and says the API rejects requests that set reasoning effort to <code>none</code> [2]. OpenAI says Sol “powers through multistep agentic workflows” and that many OpenAI employees use it as their default model [1]. Tokens per second and rate limits are not in the sources on this page.</p>
    <h2 class="ai-model-section">Context</h2>
    <p>Context is how much text the model can take in and write in one job. The API model page lists a 1,050,000-token context window, 922,000 max input tokens, and 128,000 max output tokens [2]. For requests with more than 272K input tokens, input and cache rates double and output costs 1.5 times the short-context rate for the full request [2].</p>
    <h2 class="ai-model-section">Benchmarking</h2>
    <p>Artificial Analysis, an independent tester, scores GPT-6.2 Sol 57 on its Intelligence Index v5.0.1 at max effort, 2 points above GPT-6.1 Sol and “on par with GPT-6 Astra at ~15% of the Cost per Task” [3][4]. Artificial Analysis also says Sol lost 3 points against GPT-6.1 Sol on its Coding Agent Index, driven by a 14-point drop on SWE-Atlas-QnA-AA [3]. This score is on this site’s <a href="/ai/#aiBench">benchmark table</a>.</p>
    <p>OpenAI’s own claims, not independently checked here: on Cognition’s FrontierCode 2.0, Sol comes within 0.5 points of GPT-6.2 Astra “at about one-fifth the cost (both at max),” and on OpenAI’s internal GDPval v2 it improves 5.3 points over GPT-6.1 Sol [1]. None of these tests match the columns of this site’s benchmark table.</p>
    <h2 class="ai-model-section">Access and pricing</h2>
    <p>OpenAI says that “starting today,” GPT-6.2 Astra and Sol “are available to all Plus, Pro, Enterprise, and Edu users in ChatGPT Work and Codex,” that Ultrafast comes to Pro 200 subscribers “starting with GPT-6.2 Sol,” and that developers can use <code>gpt-6.2-sol</code> through the OpenAI API [1].</p>
    <div class="ai-model-table-wrap">
      <table class="ai-model-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col"></th>
            <th scope="col">Standard, per million tokens</th>
